/* =========================================================
   LIVES OF THE FOURTEEN MA'SUMIN
   Told from the Shia sources: Kitab al-Irshad (al-Mufid), al-Kafi
   (Kitab al-Hujja), Kamal al-Din and 'Uyun Akhbar al-Rida (al-Saduq),
   Manaqib Al Abi Talib (Ibn Shahrashub), I'lam al-Wara (al-Tabrisi),
   Kashf al-Ghumma (al-Irbili), al-Luhuf (Ibn Tawus), al-Ihtijaj,
   Dala'il al-Imama, Kitab al-Ghayba (al-Tusi) and Bihar al-Anwar.
   Index matches MASUMIN.
   ========================================================= */
const LIVES = [
/* 0 — The Prophet */
{ intro: "An orphan from Mecca who became the Mercy to the Worlds. Twenty-three years of mission turned a land of idols, feuds and buried daughters into one Ummah, and he left behind two weighty things so it would never go astray.",
ch: [
["Beginnings", `<p>Muhammad ﷺ was born in Mecca on <b>17 Rabi' al-Awwal</b> in the Year of the Elephant (570 CE). His father Abd Allah died before he was born, and his mother Amina died when he was six. His grandfather Abd al-Muttalib cared for him until he was eight, and then his uncle <b>Abu Talib</b> and his wife <b>Fatima bint Asad</b> raised him as their own child.</p>
<p>He grew up in a society of idol worship, tribal pride, drinking and injustice, yet he never bowed to an idol. Mecca knew him as <b>al-Sadiq al-Amin</b>, the Truthful and the Trustworthy. At 25 he married <b>Lady Khadija</b>, the most honoured woman of Quraysh, who gave her whole wealth to his mission. He used to retreat to the cave of <b>Hira</b> to worship and reflect.</p>`],
["The mission begins", `<p>On <b>27 Rajab</b>, at the age of forty, Jibra'il came to him in Hira with the first verses: <i>"Read in the name of your Lord who created"</i> (96:1). The first to believe were <b>Khadija</b> and the young <b>Ali ibn Abi Talib</b>, who had lived with him since childhood.</p>
<p>For three years the call was quiet. Then came the command <i>"Warn your nearest kin"</i> (26:214). He gathered the sons of Abd al-Muttalib at a meal and asked who would support him and be his brother, his successor and his deputy. Only Ali, still a boy, stood up, three times. The Prophet put his hand on Ali's neck and said that this was his brother, his successor and his deputy among them.</p>`],
["The challenges", `<p>Quraysh mocked him as a madman and a sorcerer. They threw filth on him while he prayed. They tortured the weak who believed: <b>Sumayya</b> and <b>Yasir</b> were killed, the first martyrs of Islam, and <b>Bilal</b> was laid on burning sand with a rock on his chest.</p>
<p>Then Quraysh boycotted the whole clan of Banu Hashim. For about three years the Prophet, Khadija, Abu Talib and the believers were trapped in the valley of <b>Shi'b Abi Talib</b>, eating leaves. Abu Talib guarded him every night, moving him from bed to bed and putting his own sons in his place.</p>
<p>In the tenth year of the mission, both <b>Abu Talib</b> and <b>Khadija</b> died. He called it the <b>Year of Sorrow</b>. He went to Ta'if to call its people, and they set children on him who stoned him until his feet bled. Finally Quraysh agreed to kill him in his bed, with one man from every tribe so no one could take revenge.</p>`],
["How he overcame", `<p>He met hatred with patience and mercy. In Ta'if, bleeding, he prayed for guidance for those who hurt him rather than their destruction. Allah honoured him with the night journey of <b>al-Isra' and al-Mi'raj</b>.</p>
<p>On the night of the plot, <b>Ali slept in his bed</b> wrapped in his cloak, ready to die for him, and the Prophet left Mecca safely. Allah praised this in the verse <i>"And among the people is he who sells himself seeking the pleasure of Allah"</i> (2:207). He reached <b>Medina</b>, where the Ansar welcomed him. There he built the mosque, made each Muhajir a brother of an Ansari, and chose <b>Ali as his own brother</b>.</p>
<p>Quraysh attacked again and again. At <b>Badr</b> a small army of believers defeated them. At <b>Uhud</b>, when many fled, Ali stood shielding him. At <b>Khandaq</b> the trench protected Medina and Ali defeated Amr ibn Abd Wudd. At <b>Khaybar</b> the flag was given to Ali, "a man who loves Allah and His Messenger and whom Allah and His Messenger love," and the fortress fell.</p>`],
["His success", `<p>In <b>8 AH</b> he entered Mecca with ten thousand believers, almost without a fight. The same people who had tortured him stood before him, afraid. He forgave them: "Go, you are free." He and Ali cleaned the Ka'ba of its 360 idols, with Ali standing on his shoulders to break the highest ones.</p>
<p>Tribes entered Islam in crowds. At the <b>Mubahala</b> he brought only Ali, Fatima, Hasan and Husayn against the Christians of Najran. On the way back from his <b>Farewell Hajj</b>, at <b>Ghadir Khumm</b> on 18 Dhul-Hijja 10 AH, he raised Ali's hand before the pilgrims and said: <b>"Whoever I am his master (mawla), Ali is his master."</b> Then the verse came: <i>"Today I have perfected your religion for you"</i> (5:3).</p>`],
["How his life ended", `<p>In his last illness he sent an army under young Usama and told the senior companions to go with it; many delayed. When he asked for a <b>pen and paper</b> to write something so the Ummah would never go astray, one of them said that illness had overcome him and that the Book of Allah was enough. The room filled with argument and he told them to leave him.</p>
<p>He passed away on <b>28 Safar 11 AH</b>, aged 63, with his head on Ali's chest. Many Shia scholars hold that the poison he was given at Khaybar played a part. While Ali and the Ahl al-Bayt were washing and burying him, others gathered at <b>Saqifa</b> to decide the caliphate.</p>`]
],
teach: [
["Hold on to the Qur'an and the Ahl al-Bayt", "\"I am leaving among you two weighty things: the Book of Allah and my family, my Ahl al-Bayt. If you hold on to them you will never go astray.\"", "al-Kafi; Kamal al-Din"],
["Ali is the master after him", "\"Whoever I am his master, Ali is his master.\" This was announced before the pilgrims at Ghadir.", "al-Kafi; al-Irshad"],
["Character is the heart of religion", "Allah describes him: \"Indeed you are of a great character\" (68:4). He was sent as \"a mercy to the worlds\" (21:107).", "The Qur'an"],
["Seek knowledge", "\"Seeking knowledge is an obligation on every Muslim.\"", "al-Kafi"],
["Be best to your family", "\"The best of you is the one who is best to his family, and I am the best of you to my family.\"", "Man la Yahduruhu al-Faqih"],
["Honour Fatima", "\"Fatima is a part of me. Whoever hurts her has hurt me.\"", "al-Amali of al-Saduq"],
["Love Husayn", "\"Husayn is from me and I am from Husayn. Allah loves whoever loves Husayn.\"", "Kamil al-Ziyarat"],
["Care for the Ummah", "\"Whoever wakes up and does not care about the affairs of the Muslims is not one of them.\"", "al-Kafi"]
],
learn: ["Truthfulness and trust build a reputation that even enemies cannot deny.", "Patience in hardship is not weakness; the boycott and Ta'if came before the conquest.", "Forgive when you have power, as he forgave Mecca.", "Never leave the two weighty things: the Qur'an and the Ahl al-Bayt.", "A mission needs loyal helpers; look at what Khadija, Abu Talib and Ali gave."],
src: "Kitab al-Irshad; al-Kafi (Kitab al-Hujja); Kamal al-Din; Manaqib Al Abi Talib; I'lam al-Wara; Bihar al-Anwar"
},

/* 1 — Lady Fatima */
{ intro: "The daughter who was called the mother of her father. In eighteen short years she showed what a believing woman is, and her stand for the truth after the Prophet still speaks to every generation.",
ch: [
["Beginnings", `<p>Lady Fatima al-Zahra was born in Mecca on <b>20 Jumada al-Akhira</b>, five years after the mission began. Her mother was <b>Lady Khadija</b>. She opened her eyes into hardship: as a small child she lived through the boycott in <b>Shi'b Abi Talib</b>, and she lost her mother at about the age of five.</p>
<p>When Quraysh threw filth on the Prophet while he prayed, little Fatima would run to clean his back, crying. She cared for him so tenderly that he called her <b>Umm Abiha</b>, "the mother of her father." Whenever she entered, he stood up for her, kissed her hand and seated her in his place.</p>`],
["Her marriage and home", `<p>After the hijra to Medina, many wealthy men asked to marry her, and the Prophet refused them, saying her matter was with Allah. When <b>Ali</b> asked, the Prophet asked her, and her silence was her consent. The Shia reports say the Prophet told her: "Had Allah not created Ali, there would have been no match for Fatima." Ali sold his armour for the mahr, and the home was simple: a sheepskin, a pillow of palm fibre, a water skin and a hand mill.</p>
<p>She ground grain until her hands blistered. When she asked her father for a servant, he taught her instead the <b>tasbih</b> of 34, 33 and 33, which became the <b>Tasbih of Fatima</b>. Her children were <b>Hasan, Husayn, Zaynab and Umm Kulthum</b>, and a son, <b>Muhsin</b>, who was never born alive.</p>`],
["Her rank", `<p>She was one of the five under the <b>Cloak (Kisa')</b> when the verse of purification came: <i>"Allah only wishes to remove all impurity from you, O Ahl al-Bayt, and purify you completely"</i> (33:33). She was the only woman the Prophet took to the <b>Mubahala</b>. The Prophet called her the <b>leader of the women of the worlds</b> and said that her anger is his anger.</p>
<p>At night she prayed until her feet swelled. Imam Hasan said that he heard her pray all night for the neighbours, naming them one by one, and not for herself. When he asked why, she said: <b>"First the neighbour, then the house."</b></p>`],
["The challenges", `<p>Days after her father's death, while the family was in mourning, the caliphate was taken at Saqifa. Men came to her house to force Ali to give allegiance. The door was pushed in on her, she was crushed behind it, and she lost the baby <b>Muhsin</b>. Ali was dragged out.</p>
<p>Then the land of <b>Fadak</b>, which the Prophet had given her in his lifetime, was taken from her. Her witnesses, Ali and Umm Ayman, were rejected, and she was told that prophets leave no inheritance.</p>`],
["How she stood for the truth", `<p>She did not stay silent. Covered and surrounded by the women of her family, she walked into the Prophet's mosque and gave the great <b>Sermon of Fadak</b>. She explained the wisdom of prayer, fasting, justice and the Imamate, and quoted the Qur'an on the inheritance of Sulayman from Dawud and of Yahya from Zakariyya. She warned the Muslims that they had turned back on their heels.</p>
<p>She refused to speak to the two men who had wronged her until she died, so that history would record her anger. She visited the houses of the Ansar at night with Ali, reminding them of Ghadir. She wept so much for her father that a small shelter was built for her at al-Baqi', called the <b>House of Sorrows</b>.</p>`],
["How her life ended", `<p>Her injuries did not heal. On <b>3 Jumada al-Akhira 11 AH</b>, ninety-five days after her father according to the well-known report, she passed away at about eighteen years of age. She asked Ali to wash her himself, to bury her <b>at night</b>, and not to let those who had wronged her attend her funeral.</p>
<p>Ali buried her in secret. To this day her grave is unknown, a silent witness to what happened to her. Ali stood at the grave and said: "O Messenger of Allah, your daughter will tell you how your Ummah joined together to oppress her."</p>`]
],
learn: ["Serve your parents with love; she was the mother of her father.", "A simple home full of faith is richer than a palace.", "Speak the truth publicly, with dignity and knowledge, even against power.", "Think of others before yourself: first the neighbour, then the house.", "Her hidden grave teaches that truth is not erased by time."],
src: "al-Kafi; al-Ihtijaj (the Sermon of Fadak); Dala'il al-Imama; 'Ilal al-Shara'i'; al-Amali of al-Saduq and of al-Tusi; Kitab Sulaym ibn Qays; Bihar al-Anwar"
},

/* 2 — Imam Ali */
{ intro: "Born inside the Ka'ba and martyred in the mihrab. The first to believe, the bravest in battle, the most patient in being wronged, and the most just ruler Islam has known.",
ch: [
["Beginnings", `<p>Imam Ali was born on <b>13 Rajab</b>, thirty years after the Year of the Elephant, <b>inside the Ka'ba</b>. His mother Fatima bint Asad came to the House in labour; the wall opened for her and closed behind her, and she came out three days later holding her son. No one before or after was born there.</p>
<p>When a famine struck Mecca, the Prophet took young Ali into his own home. Ali later said: "I followed him as a young camel follows its mother. Every day he raised for me a sign of his character." He was the <b>first male to believe</b>, and at the feast of Dhul-'Ashira he was declared the Prophet's brother, successor and deputy.</p>`],
["With the Prophet", `<p>On the night of the hijra he slept in the Prophet's bed facing the swords of forty men. Then he brought the Prophet's family safely to Medina. In Medina the Prophet chose him as his own brother and married him to <b>Fatima</b>.</p>
<p>He was the hero of every battle: he carried the flag at <b>Badr</b>, stood firm at <b>Uhud</b> when others fled, killed Amr ibn Abd Wudd at the <b>Khandaq</b>, about whom the Prophet said "the whole of faith has gone out to face the whole of disbelief," and tore off the gate of <b>Khaybar</b>. There he received <b>Dhul-Fiqar</b>, and the call was heard: "There is no youth like Ali and no sword like Dhul-Fiqar." At <b>Ghadir</b> the Prophet raised his hand and declared him master of the believers.</p>`],
["The challenges", `<p>While Ali was burying the Prophet, the caliphate was taken at Saqifa. His house was attacked, Lady Fatima was hurt and died, and Fadak was taken. For <b>twenty-five years</b> he was kept from his right. In the Shiqshiqiyya sermon he described those years: he was patient "with a thorn in the eye and a bone in the throat, watching my inheritance being plundered."</p>
<p>When the people finally gave him allegiance in <b>35 AH</b>, he faced a corrupt state: wealth hoarded by the powerful, governors who served themselves and an Ummah used to privilege. Three wars were forced on him: <b>Jamal</b> by those who broke their pledge, <b>Siffin</b> by Muawiya who wanted the throne, and <b>Nahrawan</b> by the Khawarij who left him.</p>`],
["How he overcame", `<p>He chose patience over civil war to protect Islam. In those 25 years he collected the Qur'an, answered the questions the caliphs could not, worked farms, dug wells and gave them away as charity, and freed a thousand slaves from the work of his hands.</p>
<p>As ruler he would not buy loyalty. He gave the Arab and the non-Arab, the old companion and the new Muslim, an equal share. He put out the lamp of the treasury when a guest spoke of personal matters. He wrote to Malik al-Ashtar: <i>"People are of two kinds: your brother in religion or your equal in creation."</i> His sermons, letters and sayings were later gathered in <b>Nahj al-Balagha</b>.</p>`],
["How his life ended", `<p>At dawn on <b>19 Ramadan 40 AH</b>, as he led the Fajr prayer in the mosque of <b>Kufa</b>, the Khariji <b>Ibn Muljam</b> struck his head with a poisoned sword. His first words were: <b>"By the Lord of the Ka'ba, I have succeeded!"</b></p>
<p>Even then he asked his sons to feed his killer from his own milk and to strike him only one blow if he died. He died on <b>21 Ramadan</b>, aged 63. Hasan and Husayn buried him at night, in secret, at <b>Najaf</b>, so that his enemies could not dig up his grave. Only years later was it revealed by Imam al-Sadiq.</p>`]
],
learn: ["Loyalty to the truth even when you stand almost alone.", "Courage in battle and gentleness with the weak belong together.", "Patience can protect the religion more than a sword.", "Justice means equal rights for all, not privileges for the powerful.", "Simple living: he ate barley bread while ruling an empire."],
src: "Nahj al-Balagha; Kitab al-Irshad; al-Kafi; Manaqib Al Abi Talib; al-Amali of al-Saduq; Kashf al-Ghumma; Bihar al-Anwar"
},

/* 3 — Imam al-Hasan */
{ intro: "The first grandson, the most like the Prophet in looks. He chose a hard peace when war would have wiped out the believers, and his patience prepared the way for Karbala.",
ch: [
["Beginnings", `<p>Imam al-Hasan was born in Medina on <b>15 Ramadan 3 AH</b>, the first child of Ali and Fatima. The Prophet recited the adhan in his ear and named him Hasan, a name no Arab had used before. He looked so much like the Prophet that people who missed the Prophet would look at him.</p>
<p>The Prophet carried him on his shoulders and said: <b>"Hasan and Husayn are the masters of the youth of Paradise,"</b> and <b>"Hasan and Husayn are Imams, whether they stand or sit."</b> He was under the Cloak, and he went to the Mubahala holding the Prophet's hand.</p>`],
["Youth with his father", `<p>As a boy he lost his grandfather and then his mother, whom he had seen wronged. He grew up beside his father through the 25 years of patience. In his father's caliphate he fought at <b>Jamal</b> and <b>Siffin</b>, and was sent to Kufa to raise the army.</p>`],
["The challenges", `<p>He became Imam in <b>Ramadan 40 AH</b>, and the people of Kufa pledged to him. Muawiya, with the wealth of Syria, marched against him. Then the betrayals began. Muawiya bought commanders with gold: <b>Ubaydullah ibn Abbas</b>, the leader of his vanguard, slipped over to Muawiya at night, and many of his men followed.</p>
<p>In his own camp at Mada'in, men who had mixed with the Khawarij attacked his tent, pulled the prayer rug from under him and wounded his thigh with a spear. Letters from Kufan chiefs reached Muawiya offering to hand Hasan over, dead or alive.</p>`],
["How he overcame: the peace", `<p>He saw that a war now would end with the killing of the last loyal Shia and of the Ahl al-Bayt, and that Muawiya would then be remembered as a hero. So in <b>41 AH</b> he made a <b>peace treaty</b> with conditions: Muawiya must rule by the Qur'an and the Sunna, must not name a successor, must leave the Shia of Ali in safety, and must stop cursing Ali.</p>
<p>Some of his own followers called him "the humiliator of the believers." He answered that he had done what the Prophet did at Hudaybiyya, and what al-Khidr did when he made a hole in the boat: something that looked like a loss but saved what mattered. Soon Muawiya stood in Kufa and announced that every condition was under his feet. The world saw who he was. This was Hasan's victory: the mask fell.</p>`],
["His success", `<p>He returned to Medina and spent ten years teaching and guiding. He was famous for <b>generosity</b>: twice he gave away all his wealth, and three times he divided it in half with the poor. He walked to Hajj many times while his camels were led beside him. When a Syrian insulted him, he smiled, greeted him and offered him a home, food and help, until the man said: "Allah knows best where to place His message."</p>`],
["How his life ended", `<p>Muawiya wanted to make Yazid his heir, and Hasan stood in the way. He sent poison to Hasan's wife <b>Ja'da bint al-Ash'ath</b>, promising her money and marriage to Yazid. She poisoned him, and his liver came out in pieces. He was martyred in <b>Safar 50 AH</b>, aged about 47.</p>
<p>He had asked to be buried beside his grandfather, and that no blood be shed over it. When his coffin was brought to the Prophet's grave, Marwan and a group blocked the way and <b>arrows were shot at the coffin</b>. Husayn held back for his brother's sake, and Hasan was buried in <b>al-Baqi'</b>. In 1925 the domes of al-Baqi' were demolished; his grave still stands without a shrine.</p>`]
],
learn: ["Real courage sometimes means accepting a hard peace to save lives.", "Expose falsehood with patience; time revealed Muawiya.", "Generosity and good manners can win over even an enemy.", "Do not judge a leader by what impatient people say about him.", "Hasan's peace and Husayn's stand are one plan in two stages."],
src: "Kitab al-Irshad; al-Kafi; 'Ilal al-Shara'i'; Manaqib Al Abi Talib; Kashf al-Ghumma; Bihar al-Anwar"
},

/* 4 — Imam al-Husayn */
{ intro: "The master of the martyrs. When the Ummah's religion was being turned into a king's tool, he gave everything he had, his family, his friends and his life, and the religion lived.",
ch: [
["Beginnings", `<p>Imam al-Husayn was born in Medina on <b>3 Sha'ban 4 AH</b>. When the Prophet held him, he wept, because Jibra'il had told him what this child would face. He said: <b>"Husayn is from me and I am from Husayn. Allah loves whoever loves Husayn."</b> He would lengthen his sajda when Husayn climbed on his back.</p>
<p>He grew up under the Cloak, at the Mubahala, at the side of his father in every trial and his brother in the peace. For ten years after Hasan he lived under Muawiya, speaking against his crimes, but keeping to his brother's treaty.</p>`],
["The challenges", `<p>In <b>60 AH</b> Muawiya died and his son <b>Yazid</b> took the throne: a man who drank openly, played with dogs and monkeys, and mocked the religion. He ordered the governor of Medina to take Husayn's pledge or his head. Husayn said: <b>"Someone like me does not pledge to someone like him."</b></p>
<p>He left Medina on <b>28 Rajab</b> with his family. In Mecca, thousands of letters came from Kufa begging him to come. He sent his cousin <b>Muslim ibn Aqil</b>, who wrote that 18,000 had pledged. But Ibn Ziyad took Kufa with threats and bribes; the crowds melted away and Muslim was killed on 9 Dhul-Hijja. Agents were sent to kill Husayn even in the Haram, so on <b>8 Dhul-Hijja</b> he left Mecca, so that the sanctity of the Ka'ba would not be broken by his blood.</p>`],
["The road to Karbala", `<p>On the way he heard that Muslim had been killed and told his followers that anyone who wished could leave. <b>Al-Hurr</b> blocked his way with a thousand men, and Husayn gave them and their horses water. On <b>2 Muharram 61 AH</b> they reached <b>Karbala</b>. Umar ibn Sa'd arrived with thousands; from <b>7 Muharram</b> the water of the Euphrates was cut off.</p>
<p>On the night of Ashura he told his companions to leave in the darkness, because the enemy wanted only him. Not one left. <b>Al-Abbas</b> said: "So that we may live after you? Allah never show us that day!" Muslim ibn Awsaja said that if he had no weapon he would fight with stones.</p>`],
["Ashura", `<p>On <b>10 Muharram</b> about seventy-two men stood against an army of thousands. Al-Hurr came over to Husayn and was martyred first. The companions fell one by one, then the family: <b>Ali al-Akbar</b>, who looked like the Prophet; young <b>Qasim</b>, son of Imam Hasan; the sons of Zaynab and of Aqil; and <b>al-Abbas</b>, whose hands were cut off as he brought water for the children.</p>
<p>Husayn held up his six-month-old son <b>Ali al-Asghar</b> and asked for water for him. Harmala answered with an arrow through the baby's neck. Alone, wounded by countless arrows and swords, Husayn prayed his last prayers, and in the afternoon <b>Shimr</b> cut off his head. The tents were burned, the women and children were taken as captives, and horses were ridden over his body.</p>`],
["His success", `<p>Yazid thought he had won. But Zaynab and Imam al-Sajjad turned the captivity into a voice: in Kufa and in Yazid's own palace they told the world what had happened. Uprisings followed, the <b>Tawwabun</b> and <b>al-Mukhtar</b>, who punished the killers. The Umayyads lost their claim to religion forever.</p>
<p>His grave in Karbala became a place where millions come every year, and Arba'in is the greatest walk on earth. Imam al-Sajjad, Zaynab and the Imams after them kept the mourning alive, so that every generation would know the difference between Husayn and Yazid. As the saying goes: Islam began with Muhammad and was kept alive by Husayn.</p>`],
["How his life ended", `<p>He was martyred on <b>10 Muharram 61 AH</b>, aged 57. His head and the heads of the martyrs were carried on spears to Kufa and Damascus. Banu Asad and Imam al-Sajjad buried the bodies; an Imam is buried by an Imam. His last words in sajda were of submission to Allah: "Patience with Your decree, O Lord; there is no god but You."</p>`]
],
learn: ["Never give your pledge to falsehood, whatever the cost.", "Truth is not measured by numbers; 72 stood for all of Islam.", "Give everyone a chance to return, as he did with al-Hurr.", "Loyalty like that of al-Abbas and the companions is the true test of love.", "Keeping Karbala alive through mourning is keeping the religion alive."],
src: "Kitab al-Irshad; al-Luhuf (Ibn Tawus); Kamil al-Ziyarat; al-Amali of al-Saduq; Tuhaf al-'Uqul; Manaqib Al Abi Talib; Bihar al-Anwar"
},

/* 5 — Imam Zayn al-Abidin */
{ intro: "The survivor of Karbala. In an age of terror, he rebuilt the Shia with tears, du'a and secret charity, and left the Psalms of the Ahl al-Bayt for the world.",
ch: [
["Beginnings", `<p>Imam Ali ibn al-Husayn, called <b>Zayn al-Abidin</b> (the ornament of the worshippers) and <b>al-Sajjad</b> (the one who prostrates much), was born in Medina in <b>Sha'ban 38 AH</b>. He grew up in the house of his grandfather Ali and his uncle Hasan, and saw the betrayals of Kufa with his own eyes.</p>`],
["Karbala and captivity", `<p>At Karbala he was so ill that he could not fight; this was Allah's plan to keep the line of the Imamate alive. After Ashura the soldiers wanted to kill him too, and his aunt <b>Zaynab</b> threw herself over him and said they would have to kill her first.</p>
<p>He was taken in chains with the women and children to <b>Kufa</b> and then to <b>Damascus</b>. In Yazid's palace he gave a sermon: <b>"I am the son of Mecca and Mina, I am the son of Zamzam and Safa... I am the son of the one who was killed thirsty in Karbala."</b> The crowd wept, and Yazid had the adhan called to stop him, but the adhan itself spoke of his grandfather.</p>`],
["The challenges", `<p>He returned to a frightened Medina. The Shia had been killed or scattered, and few dared to come near him. In <b>63 AH</b> Yazid's army sacked Medina in the <b>Battle of al-Harra</b>, killing and violating its people, and then attacked the Ka'ba. Later the tyrant <b>al-Hajjaj</b> hunted down anyone who loved Ali. Open teaching was impossible.</p>`],
["How he overcame", `<p>He chose weapons the tyrants could not take away. The first was <b>du'a</b>: through his supplications he taught belief, ethics and the right of the Ahl al-Bayt. They were gathered as <b>al-Sahifa al-Sajjadiyya</b>, the "Psalms of the Ahl al-Bayt." He also wrote the <b>Treatise of Rights (Risalat al-Huquq)</b>, setting out the rights of Allah, of the self, of parents, children, neighbours, teachers and even enemies.</p>
<p>The second was <b>tears</b>. For years he wept for his father; when water was brought to him he would weep and say: "How can I not weep, when my father was killed thirsty?" Every tear reminded people of Karbala.</p>
<p>The third was <b>hidden charity</b>. At night he carried sacks of bread on his back to about a hundred poor families of Medina, his face covered. They only knew who it was when it stopped at his death, and the marks were found on his back. He bought slaves, taught them and freed them, often on Eid al-Fitr. In al-Harra he even sheltered the family of Marwan, his enemy.</p>`],
["His success", `<p>Slowly the Shia came back to him, and his students and freed slaves spread his teachings. One year at the Ka'ba, the crowds parted for him while the Umayyad prince Hisham could not reach the Black Stone. When Hisham pretended not to know who he was, the poet <b>al-Farazdaq</b> recited his famous poem: "This is the one whose steps the valley of Mecca knows, and the House knows him, and the Haram and the land outside it."</p>`],
["How his life ended", `<p>The Umayyad caliph <b>al-Walid ibn Abd al-Malik</b> had him poisoned. He was martyred in Medina in <b>Muharram 95 AH</b>, aged 57, and buried in <b>al-Baqi'</b> beside his uncle Imam al-Hasan.</p>`]
],
learn: ["When you cannot speak openly, du'a can still teach the truth.", "Remember the oppressed; his tears kept Karbala alive.", "Give in secret; the right hand should not know what the left gives.", "Know and give everyone their rights, even those you dislike.", "Rebuilding after loss is a form of jihad."],
src: "al-Sahifa al-Sajjadiyya; Risalat al-Huquq (Tuhaf al-'Uqul); Kitab al-Irshad; al-Luhuf; al-Kafi; Manaqib Al Abi Talib; Bihar al-Anwar"
},

/* 6 — Imam al-Baqir */
{ intro: "The splitter of knowledge. The Prophet sent him greetings across fifty years, and he opened the great school of the Ahl al-Bayt.",
ch: [
["Beginnings", `<p>Imam Muhammad ibn Ali was born in Medina on <b>1 Rajab 57 AH</b>. His mother Fatima was the daughter of Imam al-Hasan, so he was the first Imam descended from both Hasan and Husayn. As a boy of about four he was at <b>Karbala</b> and walked with the captives.</p>
<p>The Prophet had told the companion <b>Jabir ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari</b> that he would live to meet a son of Husayn named Muhammad, "who will split open knowledge (yabqur al-'ilm)," and asked Jabir to give him his salam. Jabir, old and blind, searched Medina until he found the boy, kissed him and delivered the greeting of the Prophet.</p>`],
["The challenges", `<p>He became Imam in <b>95 AH</b>. The Umayyads still ruled; the Shia had lost much of their knowledge of fiqh and hadith after decades of fear. People had forgotten even how the Prophet prayed and performed Hajj. Caliph <b>Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik</b> hated him and once had him brought to Damascus to humiliate him before the court.</p>`],
["How he overcame", `<p>As the Umayyads grew weaker and fought among themselves, he used the opening to teach. He sat in the Prophet's mosque and answered questions on tafsir, fiqh, theology and history. Great students gathered around him: <b>Zurara ibn A'yan, Muhammad ibn Muslim</b>, who heard thirty thousand hadith from him, <b>Abu Basir</b>, <b>Burayd</b> and <b>Jabir al-Ju'fi</b>.</p>
<p>In Damascus, Hisham's men mocked him and challenged him to shoot arrows, hoping he would fail. He shot nine arrows; each one split the one before it in the target. The court fell silent. He answered the questions of a Christian scholar until the man said there was no one more learned than him.</p>`],
["His success", `<p>He laid the foundation of <b>Shia fiqh</b>: much of what we know about wudu, prayer, Hajj and the laws comes from him and his son. He explained that the Imamate is a covenant from Allah, not a seat for the strongest. The school he began would grow under al-Sadiq into thousands of students.</p>`],
["How his life ended", `<p>He was poisoned on the orders of Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik and was martyred on <b>7 Dhul-Hijja 114 AH</b>, aged 57. He left money in his will for mourning gatherings to be held for him at <b>Mina</b> during Hajj for ten years, so that pilgrims from every land would ask and learn about the Ahl al-Bayt. He was buried in <b>al-Baqi'</b>.</p>`]
],
learn: ["Use every opening to spread knowledge.", "Teach people to act, not only to repeat.", "Answer insults with skill and calm, as in Hisham's court.", "Knowledge of the religion must be taken from its true source, the Ahl al-Bayt.", "Even a will can serve the faith, like his gatherings at Mina."],
src: "Kitab al-Irshad; al-Kafi; Dala'il al-Imama; Manaqib Al Abi Talib; I'lam al-Wara; Bihar al-Anwar"
},

/* 7 — Imam al-Sadiq */
{ intro: "The teacher of thousands. Between the fall of one dynasty and the rise of another, he built the school whose name the Shia carry: the Ja'fari school.",
ch: [
["Beginnings", `<p>Imam Ja'far ibn Muhammad was born in Medina on <b>17 Rabi' al-Awwal 83 AH</b>, the same date as the Prophet's birth. He grew up for twelve years with his grandfather Imam al-Sajjad and then studied at his father al-Baqir's side as the school began.</p>`],
["The challenges", `<p>He became Imam in <b>114 AH</b>. The Umayyads were collapsing, and in <b>132 AH</b> the <b>Abbasids</b> took power, claiming to avenge the family of the Prophet. Many urged him to seize the moment. <b>Abu Salama</b>, a leader of the revolution, sent him a letter offering him the caliphate; the Imam held it over a lamp and burned it, saying this man was not one of their Shia. He knew the movement was built on ambition, not on the Imamate.</p>
<p>Once in power, the Abbasids were as cruel as the Umayyads. <b>Al-Mansur</b> saw the Imam as his greatest threat. He summoned him to Iraq again and again, insulted him and planned to kill him. His governor in Medina was ordered to set the Imam's house on fire.</p>`],
["How he overcame", `<p>While the two dynasties fought, he opened the doors of learning wider than ever. Shaykh al-Mufid records that four thousand reliable narrators took knowledge from him. His students included <b>Zurara, Muhammad ibn Muslim, Hisham ibn al-Hakam</b>, the master debater, <b>Aban ibn Taghlib</b> and <b>al-Mufaddal</b>, to whom he dictated lessons on the signs of Allah in creation. They studied fiqh, tafsir, theology, ethics and the natural world.</p>
<p>He protected the Shia through <b>taqiyya</b>, careful speech, so that they could survive, and he trained them to be an example: <b>"Be an adornment for us and not a disgrace."</b> He set up a network of trusted representatives to collect khums and answer questions.</p>`],
["His success", `<p>His teachings fill the great books of hadith; most of what is in al-Kafi and the Four Books comes from him and his father. That is why Shia fiqh is called <b>Ja'fari</b>. Even scholars outside his school came to his gatherings to learn.</p>`],
["How his life ended", `<p>Al-Mansur had him poisoned. He was martyred on <b>25 Shawwal 148 AH</b>, aged 65. Al-Mansur then ordered the governor to find the Imam's chosen successor and kill him. But the Imam had named <b>five</b> people in his will: al-Mansur himself, the governor, his son Abd Allah, his son <b>Musa</b> and his wife Hamida. The plot failed, and Musa was protected. He was buried in <b>al-Baqi'</b>.</p>`]
],
learn: ["Do not jump at power that is offered for the wrong reasons.", "Knowledge is the strongest defence of the faith.", "Be an adornment for the Ahl al-Bayt in how you live.", "Wisdom and planning protect the truth, as his will protected Musa.", "Respect the mind: he taught that intellect is Allah's inner proof."],
src: "Kitab al-Irshad; al-Kafi; Tuhaf al-'Uqul; Tawhid al-Mufaddal; Manaqib Al Abi Talib; Bihar al-Anwar"
},

/* 8 — Imam al-Kazim */
{ intro: "The one who swallowed his anger. He spent years in the dungeons of Harun al-Rashid, turning a prison cell into a place of worship.",
ch: [
["Beginnings", `<p>Imam Musa ibn Ja'far was born at <b>al-Abwa'</b>, between Mecca and Medina, on <b>7 Safar 128 AH</b>. He was called <b>al-Kazim</b> because he swallowed his anger and answered harm with kindness, and <b>Bab al-Hawa'ij</b>, the door of needs, because so many prayers are answered through him.</p>
<p>When a man in Medina insulted him and his family, his companions wanted to punish him. The Imam forbade them, rode out to the man's farm, greeted him kindly and paid him more than his crop was worth. The man became his devoted follower.</p>`],
["The challenges", `<p>He became Imam in <b>148 AH</b> and lived under four Abbasid caliphs: al-Mansur, al-Mahdi, al-Hadi and <b>Harun al-Rashid</b>. Harun ruled a huge empire, yet he feared one man in Medina. Once, at the Prophet's grave, Harun greeted the Prophet as "cousin" to show off his kinship; the Imam greeted him as <b>"father"</b>, and Harun's face changed.</p>
<p>Harun once offered to return <b>Fadak</b>. The Imam said he would accept it only with its true borders, and described the borders of the whole Muslim lands. Harun understood: the real Fadak was the right to lead the Ummah.</p>`],
["Prison", `<p>In <b>179 AH</b> Harun had him arrested while he was praying beside the Prophet's grave. He was held for years: first in <b>Basra</b> under Isa ibn Ja'far, then in <b>Baghdad</b> under al-Fadl ibn al-Rabi', al-Fadl ibn Yahya and finally the cruel <b>al-Sindi ibn Shahak</b>, in a dark underground cell.</p>
<p>He did not complain. He thanked Allah: "O Allah, I used to ask You for free time to worship You, and You have given it to me." He spent his nights in long sajdas. Jailers who were ordered to watch him came to love him, and the governor of Basra wrote to Harun asking to be relieved because he could find nothing against him.</p>`],
["How he overcame", `<p>From prison he still guided the Shia through his <b>wakils</b> (representatives), who collected khums and answered questions. His patience and character turned enemies into followers. He trained his son <b>Ali al-Rida</b> to carry the Imamate after him.</p>`],
["How his life ended", `<p>Harun ordered him poisoned. He was martyred in al-Sindi's prison on <b>25 Rajab 183 AH</b>, aged 55. His body was laid on the bridge of Baghdad with the words "This is the Imam of the Rafida", to humiliate the Shia. But the people came out in thousands to carry him with honour. He was buried in the cemetery of Quraysh, today's <b>Kadhimiya</b>, where his shrine shines with golden domes.</p>`]
],
learn: ["Swallow your anger and answer harm with good.", "Prison of the body cannot imprison the soul.", "Use hardship as time for worship.", "Good character turns enemies into friends.", "Stay connected to the Imam's guidance, even through his representatives."],
src: "Kitab al-Irshad; al-Kafi; 'Uyun Akhbar al-Rida; Manaqib Al Abi Talib; Kashf al-Ghumma; Bihar al-Anwar"
},

/* 9 — Imam al-Rida */
{ intro: "The Imam of the stranger land. Forced into the court of al-Ma'mun, he turned it into a stage for the truth, and Mashhad became the heart of the Shia world.",
ch: [
["Beginnings", `<p>Imam Ali ibn Musa was born in Medina on <b>11 Dhul-Qa'da 148 AH</b>. He became Imam in <b>183 AH</b>, when his father died in Harun's prison. For years he taught openly in Medina, answering questions in the Prophet's mosque; scholars said they had never seen anyone more learned.</p>`],
["The challenges", `<p>After Harun, his sons <b>al-Amin</b> and <b>al-Ma'mun</b> fought a civil war, and al-Ma'mun killed his brother. Facing revolts, many of them by descendants of Ali, al-Ma'mun made a clever plan: he would bring the Imam to his capital at Merv and name him <b>heir to the caliphate</b>. That would win the Shia, keep the Imam under watch, and stain his name with the government's crimes.</p>
<p>In <b>201 AH</b> the Imam was forced to leave Medina. Before going he gathered his family to weep for him, knowing he would not return. At first he refused al-Ma'mun's offer, until he was threatened with death.</p>`],
["How he overcame", `<p>He accepted only on condition that he would <b>not</b> appoint or dismiss anyone, judge or give orders. So he stayed free of the government's sins.</p>
<p>In <b>Nishapur</b>, tens of thousands of people and scholars with ink pots begged for a hadith. From his litter he narrated, through his fathers, from the Prophet, from Allah: <b>"'There is no god but Allah' is My fortress; whoever enters My fortress is safe from My punishment."</b> Then he added: <b>"With its conditions, and I am one of its conditions."</b> This is the famous <b>Golden Chain</b> hadith.</p>
<p>Al-Ma'mun gathered the greatest scholars of the Christians, Jews, Zoroastrians and philosophers to debate him, hoping he would fail. He answered each from their own scriptures in their own language, until they admitted his knowledge. When al-Ma'mun asked him to lead the Eid prayer, he walked out barefoot reciting takbir, and the whole city followed him weeping. Al-Ma'mun, afraid, called him back.</p>`],
["How his life ended", `<p>Al-Ma'mun saw that his plan had failed: the Imam's honour grew and his own shrank. He poisoned him, reportedly with grapes, in <b>Tus</b>, at the end of <b>Safar 203 AH</b>. The Imam was 55. He was buried in the same building as Harun al-Rashid. Today that place is <b>Mashhad</b>, the city of the shrine; Harun's grave is forgotten beneath the Imam's dome.</p>
<p>The Imam had said: "Whoever visits me in my exile, I will come to him on three occasions on the Day of Judgement," to rescue him.</p>`]
],
learn: ["You can stay pure even in the court of tyrants, by setting clear conditions.", "Tawhid and the Imamate go together: 'I am one of its conditions.'", "Answer every faith with knowledge and respect.", "Fame given by the powerful is a test, not a reward.", "Visit him; his shrine is a refuge for the believers."],
src: "'Uyun Akhbar al-Rida; Kitab al-Irshad; al-Kafi; Kamal al-Din; al-Tawhid of al-Saduq; Bihar al-Anwar"
},

/* 10 — Imam al-Jawad */
{ intro: "The youngest Imam. At about eight years old he silenced the chief judge of the empire, proving that knowledge of the Imam is a gift from Allah, not a matter of age.",
ch: [
["Beginnings", `<p>Imam Muhammad ibn Ali was born in Medina on <b>10 Rajab 195 AH</b>. His father al-Rida had been waiting long for a son; he called him the most blessed child born for the Shia. He was called <b>al-Jawad</b>, the generous, and <b>al-Taqi</b>, the pious.</p>`],
["The challenges", `<p>He became Imam in <b>203 AH</b>, when he was only about <b>eight years old</b>. Some Shia were confused: how could a child be the Imam? The scholars of the court mocked the idea.</p>
<p>Al-Ma'mun arranged a great gathering in Baghdad, and the chief judge <b>Yahya ibn Aktham</b> asked him a question: what is the ruling on a pilgrim in ihram who kills game? The young Imam answered with a question: was it inside or outside the Haram, was he learned or ignorant, did he do it on purpose or by mistake, was he free or a slave, a child or an adult, was it his first time or a repeat, was the game a bird or something else, was he sorry or insisting, at night or by day, in Hajj or Umra? Yahya was speechless. Then the Imam answered every case.</p>`],
["How he overcame", `<p>The Shia saw that the Imamate is not measured in years: Allah gave Yahya the wisdom as a child and Isa spoke from the cradle. He answered thousands of questions, sometimes in a single sitting, and strengthened the network of representatives.</p>
<p>Al-Ma'mun married him to his daughter <b>Umm al-Fadl</b> to keep him close and watched. The Imam lived mostly in Medina, generous to all, but the marriage was unhappy, and Umm al-Fadl was jealous of the mother of his son al-Hadi.</p>`],
["How his life ended", `<p>The next caliph, <b>al-Mu'tasim</b>, called him to Baghdad in <b>220 AH</b>. Through Umm al-Fadl he was poisoned, and he was martyred at the end of <b>Dhul-Qa'da 220 AH</b>, only about <b>25 years old</b>, the youngest of the Imams to die. He was buried in <b>Kadhimiya</b> beside his grandfather al-Kazim.</p>`]
],
learn: ["Do not judge people by their age; judge them by their knowledge and character.", "Answer questions with careful thinking: one question can have many cases.", "Generosity is a mark of the Ahl al-Bayt.", "Political marriages and favours from tyrants do not change the truth.", "Allah's proofs are present in every age."],
src: "Kitab al-Irshad; al-Kafi; al-Ihtijaj; Tuhaf al-'Uqul; Manaqib Al Abi Talib; Bihar al-Anwar"
},

/* 11 — Imam al-Hadi */
{ intro: "The Imam held in a military camp for twenty years. Under constant watch he taught the Shia how to visit their Imams, and the Ziyara Jami'a Kabira is his gift to us.",
ch: [
["Beginnings", `<p>Imam Ali ibn Muhammad was born near Medina in <b>Dhul-Hijja 212 AH</b>. Like his father, he became Imam as a child, at about eight, in <b>220 AH</b>, and the Shia accepted him because they had already seen his father.</p>`],
["The challenges", `<p>The caliph <b>al-Mutawakkil</b> was one of the worst enemies of the Ahl al-Bayt. In <b>233 AH</b> he sent an army commander to bring the Imam from Medina to <b>Samarra</b>, the army city, and kept him there under watch for about twenty years. The Imam's neighbourhood was the military camp, al-'Askar.</p>
<p>Al-Mutawakkil ordered the <b>shrine of Imam Husayn</b> to be destroyed, the land ploughed and flooded, and punished anyone who visited it. His soldiers raided the Imam's house at night looking for weapons and money. They found him in a woollen garment, on the sand, facing the qibla and reciting the Qur'an.</p>`],
["How he overcame", `<p>Once al-Mutawakkil had him brought into a drinking party and offered him wine. The Imam refused and, when ordered to recite poetry, recited verses about kings who once lived in palaces guarded by armies and are now eaten by worms in their graves. Al-Mutawakkil wept until his beard was wet, and the party ended.</p>
<p>Cut off from most people, he built a strong network of <b>wakils</b> (representatives) across the lands, preparing the Shia for a time when they could not reach the Imam directly. He taught the <b>Ziyara al-Jami'a al-Kabira</b>, the great ziyara that explains who the Imams are, and the <b>Ziyara al-Ghadir</b> for Imam Ali.</p>`],
["How his life ended", `<p>Al-Mutawakkil was killed by his own son and soldiers. Under a later caliph, <b>al-Mu'tazz</b>, the Imam was poisoned and martyred in Samarra on <b>3 Rajab 254 AH</b>, aged about 42. He was buried in his house in Samarra, the <b>shrine of al-Askariyyayn</b>; its golden dome was bombed in 2006 and has since been rebuilt.</p>`]
],
learn: ["Stay firm in worship even when watched and pressured.", "A few true words can melt the heart of a tyrant.", "Prepare people for hard times before they come.", "Ziyara is a school of belief; learn its words.", "Tyrants pass; the shrines of the Imams remain."],
src: "Kitab al-Irshad; al-Kafi; Man la Yahduruhu al-Faqih (al-Ziyara al-Jami'a); Manaqib Al Abi Talib; Bihar al-Anwar"
},

/* 12 — Imam al-Askari */
{ intro: "The Imam of the hidden preparation. In six short years under house arrest, he guarded the birth of the Mahdi and readied the Shia for the Occultation.",
ch: [
["Beginnings", `<p>Imam al-Hasan ibn Ali was born in Medina on <b>8 Rabi' al-Thani 232 AH</b>. As a child he was taken with his father to <b>Samarra</b>, and grew up in the military camp, which is why he is called <b>al-Askari</b>.</p>`],
["The challenges", `<p>He became Imam in <b>254 AH</b>. The Abbasids knew from the hadith that the Twelfth Imam, the <b>Mahdi</b>, would be his son, and that he would end the rule of tyrants. So they watched his house closely, put spies among his servants and searched for any sign of a child. He was required to appear at the palace regularly, and he was put in prison more than once.</p>`],
["How he overcame", `<p>He met the danger with careful planning. When his son <b>Muhammad</b> was born on <b>15 Sha'ban 255 AH</b>, he kept the birth secret and showed the child only to a few trusted Shia, so they could be witnesses.</p>
<p>He limited his public meetings and guided the Shia through <b>letters</b> and <b>representatives</b>, such as the trusted <b>Uthman ibn Sa'id</b>, training them to receive guidance without seeing the Imam. In prison his worship changed the jailers: the guards placed over him to torment him became people of prayer and fasting.</p>
<p>When the philosopher al-Kindi began writing a book claiming the Qur'an contradicted itself, the Imam sent one of al-Kindi's students to ask him a single question: could the speaker of the Qur'an have meant something other than what al-Kindi thought? Al-Kindi realised his mistake and burned the book.</p>`],
["How his life ended", `<p>The caliph <b>al-Mu'tamid</b> had him poisoned. He was martyred on <b>8 Rabi' al-Awwal 260 AH</b>, aged only <b>28</b>. As his brother Ja'far stepped forward to lead the funeral prayer, a young boy came out, pulled his cloak and said: <b>"Step back, uncle. I have more right to pray over my father."</b> The boy led the prayer, and the Shia knew their new Imam. He was buried beside his father in Samarra.</p>`]
],
learn: ["Planning and secrecy are sometimes needed to protect the truth.", "Hold on to guidance even when you cannot see the Imam.", "Your worship can change the people around you.", "One wise question can defeat a whole book of doubts.", "Short lives can carry great missions."],
src: "Kamal al-Din; Kitab al-Ghayba of al-Tusi; Kitab al-Irshad; al-Kafi; Manaqib Al Abi Talib; Bihar al-Anwar"
},

/* 13 — Imam al-Mahdi */
{ intro: "The Imam of our time. Alive, hidden, and waiting for Allah's command to fill the earth with justice as it is filled with oppression.",
ch: [
["Beginnings", `<p>Imam Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, <b>al-Mahdi</b>, was born in Samarra on <b>15 Sha'ban 255 AH</b>. His mother was <b>Narjis</b>. Like the birth of Prophet Musa, his birth was hidden from the tyrants who were waiting to kill him. His father showed him only to trusted Shia and said: "This is your Imam after me."</p>`],
["The challenges", `<p>He became Imam in <b>260 AH</b> at about five years old. The Abbasids raided the house after his father's death to find him. Some false claimants, like his uncle Ja'far, tried to take the Imamate.</p>`],
["The Minor Occultation", `<p>Allah kept him hidden. For about <b>69 years</b> (260 to 329 AH) the Shia reached him through <b>four special deputies</b>, one after another: <b>Uthman ibn Sa'id, Muhammad ibn Uthman, al-Husayn ibn Rawh</b> and <b>Ali ibn Muhammad al-Samuri</b>. They carried questions, khums and letters, and brought back his answers, called the <b>tawqi'at</b>.</p>
<p>In one letter he told the Shia: <b>"As for the events that will happen, refer in them to the narrators of our hadith, for they are my proof upon you, and I am Allah's proof upon them."</b> This is the root of following the <b>maraji'</b> today.</p>`],
["The Major Occultation", `<p>In <b>329 AH</b> he sent a last letter to al-Samuri saying there would be no more special deputies, and the <b>Major Occultation</b> began. He is alive, sees the believers, and joins them in Hajj without being recognised. He said: <b>"We do not neglect your affairs, and we do not forget you."</b> People benefit from him as they benefit from the sun behind the clouds.</p>`],
["His return", `<p>He will reappear when Allah permits, with Prophet Isa praying behind him, and will <b>fill the earth with justice and fairness as it was filled with oppression and injustice</b>. He will take revenge for the blood of Husayn. His life has not ended; his story is still being written, and we are part of it.</p>`]
],
learn: ["Intizar (awaiting) means preparing ourselves, not just waiting.", "Follow a qualified marja', as he commanded.", "Pray for his safe return: Du'a al-Faraj, Du'a al-'Ahd and Du'a al-Nudba.", "Give charity for his protection and remember him on Fridays.", "Live so that he would be pleased if he appeared today."],
src: "Kamal al-Din; Kitab al-Ghayba of al-Tusi; Kitab al-Ghayba of al-Nu'mani; al-Kafi; al-Ihtijaj; Kitab al-Irshad"
}
];
